WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.191
An Express service often begins as
something small:

2
00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:11.561
a handful of internal API routes, a login
callback, a status endpoint,

3
00:00:11.641 --> 00:00:13.666
or a webhook receiver.

4
00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:20.569
Over time, the same service may become a
public application with real users,

5
00:00:20.649 --> 00:00:27.366
admin routes, production data, external
integrations, session cookies,

6
00:00:27.446 --> 00:00:32.563
npm middleware, reverse proxies, and CI/CD
deployment.

7
00:00:32.643 --> 00:00:39.253
That growth is normal, but it changes the
security meaning of every route.

8
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:46.049
What began as convenient request handling
becomes a boundary around data,

9
00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:49.114
identity, and business actions.

10
00:00:49.194 --> 00:00:52.499
Express is intentionally flexible.

11
00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:59.189
It gives developers routing, middleware,
request and response objects,

12
00:00:59.269 --> 00:01:03.960
and a thin structure for building web
applications.

13
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.599
That flexibility is useful,

14
00:01:06.679 --> 00:01:13.502
but it also means security behavior
depends heavily on application code and

15
00:01:13.582 --> 00:01:15.074
configuration.

16
00:01:15.154 --> 00:01:21.764
Missing middleware, inconsistent route
guards, unsafe input handling,

17
00:01:21.844 --> 00:01:28.560
broad CORS settings, weak cookie
attributes, verbose production errors,

18
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.783
dependency risk, and unsafe runtime
settings are common failure modes because

19
00:01:35.863 --> 00:01:41.407
Express will generally do what the
application tells it to do.

20
00:01:41.487 --> 00:01:47.777
A secure Express application should make
request handling explicit,

21
00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:52.228
limited, validated, observable, and
testable.

22
00:01:52.308 --> 00:01:59.770
The goal is not to make Express
complicated or slow down development with ceremony.

23
00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:05.821
The goal is to make security expectations
visible and repeatable.

24
00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:09.525
Routes should be grouped by trust level,

25
00:02:09.605 --> 00:02:13.763
middleware should be applied
intentionally,

26
00:02:13.843 --> 00:02:18.321
input should be validated before business
logic,

27
00:02:18.401 --> 00:02:25.117
responses should be shaped deliberately,
secrets should stay protected,

28
00:02:25.197 --> 00:02:32.340
and operators should be able to see when
the application behaves unexpectedly.
